.TH SHSELECT 1 "17.11.2026" "shselect 1.0" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
shselect \- terminal selector and pager

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shselect
.RB [ \-bcghlmnsv ]
.RB [ \-r\ height ]
.RB [ \-p\ text ]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B shselect
is a pager and selector.
It reads input from a pipe or standard input and displays the content in an
interactive terminal interface.
The user can move through the list, select entries, and return the selected
line(s) or line number(s) to the calling script.

The tool is intended for use in POSIX shell scripts where an interactive
selector is useful.
Depending on the options,
.B shselect
can return selected lines, their line numbers, or both.
When multiselect mode is enabled, multiple entries may be marked and returned.

.B shselect
is part of the
.B shui-tools
package.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-b
Display the bottom bar in reverse video.

.TP
.B \-c
Show cursor in first column instead of reverse video line.

.TP
.B \-g
Disable the bottom bar.

.TP
.B \-h
Show usage information.

.TP
.B \-l
Behave like an ANSI code aware pager.

.TP
.B \-m
Enable multiselect mode.
When enabled, entries may be marked or unmarked.

.TP
.B \-n
Return only the line number of the selected entry.

.TP
.BI "\-p " text
Set the bottom bar text.

.TP
.BI "\-r " lines
Set the height of the selection area in lines.
A value of 0 uses the entire terminal height.
The default is 4.

.TP
.B \-s
Return only the selected line itself.

By default, both the line number and the line content are returned.

.TP
.B \-v
Leaving with
.B x
or
.B q
also returns the string the cursor was on.

.SH INTERACTIVE KEYS
While viewing a list, the following keys are available:

.TS
tab(|);
l l.
Right, PageDown|Move forward one window
Left, PageUp|Move backward one window
Down|Move down one line
Up|Move up one line
HOME|Move to first line
END|Move to last line
_
Enter|Select and exit
q|Exit without selection (return 0)
x|Exit without selection (return 10)
m, s, +|Toggle mark (multiselect mode only)
.TE

Exiting with
.B x
returns 10 and can be used as a "go back" signal in scripts.

.SH EXAMPLES
Select a file from a directory listing:

.PP
.nf
ls -a . | shselect -s
.fi

Select from a list of strings:

.PP
.nf
printf "%s\\n%s\\n%s\\n" "One" "Two" "Three" | shselect -sb
.fi

Possible usage in a shell script:

.PP
.nf
key=$(printf "${radiostations}" | shselect -bs)
.fi

Use as a pager:

.PP
.nf
shselect -l < file.txt
.fi

